## Architecture Considerations

| Component | Implementation |
|-----------|----------------|
| **Trigger** | S3 Event Notification → Lambda → Step Functions |
| **Textract** | AnalyzeDocument with FORMS and TABLES feature types |
| **Comprehend** | Custom classifier for doc type + DetectPIIEntities |
| **Redaction** | Lambda using PDF library to mask coordinates |
| **Storage** | Separate buckets for original and redacted with KMS encryption |
| **Indexing** | DynamoDB with contractId as partition key |
| **Error Handling** | Try-catch per state, DLQ for failed executions |
| **Cost Control** | S3 lifecycle policies, CloudWatch budgets, payload limits |
| **Data Residency** | Regional endpoints, bucket policies enforcing region |
| **IAM** | Role-based access with least privilege scope |

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of end-to-end AWS data pipelines using AI services, speaker diarization, secure data handling, error management, IAM least-privilege principles, and cost optimization for beginner-level implementations.

## Key Concepts

- Event-driven architecture: S3 → Lambda → Amazon Transcribe → DynamoDB
- Transcribe diarization for per-speaker transcript segmentation
- Multi-format output storage (full + individual speaker transcripts)
- DynamoDB indexing strategy with composite keys
- IAM security with least-privilege access patterns
- Error handling with retries and dead-letter queues
- Data privacy through KMS encryption and access controls
- Cost monitoring and optimization for short audio clips

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example Lambda: S3 event handling to start Transcribe
exports.handler = async (event) => {
  const record = event.Records[0];
  const bucket = record.s3.bucket.name;
  const key = record.s3.object.key;

  // Start Transcribe job with diarization
  const transcribeParams = {
    TranscriptionJobName: `interview-${Date.now()}`,
    LanguageCode: 'en-US',
    MediaFormat: key.split('.').pop(),
    Media: { MediaFileUri: `s3://${bucket}/${key}` },
    Settings: {
      ShowSpeakerLabels: true,
      MaxSpeakerLabels: 10
    },
    OutputBucketName: process.env.OUTPUT_BUCKET
  };

  await transcribe.startTranscriptionJob(transcribeParams).promise();
};
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to design a practical, event-driven data pipeline with CSV parsing, per-record processing, PII masking, and cost-conscious AWS resource usage.

## Key Concepts
- S3 event triggers and Lambda functions
- CSV parsing and per-message processing
- Amazon Comprehend: sentiment analysis and entity detection
- PII masking rules and data privacy compliance
- S3 encryption, IAM least privilege, and dead-letter queues (DLQ)
- DynamoDB indexing for conversation metadata

## Code Example
```javascript
function maskPII(text) {
  const emailRegex = /\b[A-Za-z0-9._%+-]+@[A-Za-z0-9.-]+\.[A-Z|a-z]{2,}\b/g;
  const phoneRegex = /\b\d{3}[-.]?\d{3}[-.]?\d{4}\b/g;
  return text
    .replace(emailRegex, '[EMAIL_REDACTED]')
    .replace(phoneRegex, '[PHONE_REDACTED]');
}

async function processTranscript(record) {
  const bucket = record.s3.bucket.name;
  const key = decodeURIComponent(record.s3.object.key.replace(/\+/g, ' '));
  const response = await s3.getObject({ Bucket: bucket, Key: key }).promise();
  const csvContent = response.Body.toString();
  const rows = parseCSV(csvContent);
  const messages = rows.map(row => ({
    role: row.role,
    timestamp: row.timestamp,
    message: maskPII(row.message)
  }));
  return messages;
}

async function analyzeSentiment(text) {
  const result = await comprehend.detectSentiment({
    Text: text,
    LanguageCode: 'en'
  }).promise();
  return result.Sentiment;
}

async function detectEntities(text) {
  const result = await comprehend.detectEntities({
    Text: text,
    LanguageCode: 'en'
  }).promise();
  return result.Entities;
}
```

